    question: "What is the difference between a tree and a graph?",
    slug: "tree-vs-graph",
    shortDescription: "Compare tree and graph.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA tree is a connected, acyclic graph with N nodes and N-1 edges. A graph can have cycles and may be disconnected. Trees have a hierarchical structure, graphs can have arbitrary connections. Key points to remember: - Tree: exactly one path between any two nodes, no cycles. - Graph: may have multiple paths, cycles, can be disconnected. - Trees are special cases of graphs. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a tree and a graph and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A tree is a connected, acyclic graph with N nodes and N-1 edges.\n2. A graph can have cycles and may be disconnected.\n3. Trees have a hierarchical structure, graphs can have arbitrary connections.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A tree is a connected, acyclic graph with N nodes and N-1 edges.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A tree is a connected, acyclic graph with N nodes and N-1 edges.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
