    question: "What is the difference between a stack and a queue?",
    slug: "stack-vs-queue",
    shortDescription: "Compare stack and queue.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nStack is LIFO, queue is FIFO. Stack uses push/pop; queue uses enqueue/dequeue. Stacks are used for recursion, queues for BFS. Key points to remember: - Stack: add/remove from same end (top). - Queue: add at one end (rear), remove from other (front). - Both can be implemented with arrays or linked lists. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a stack and a queue and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Stack is LIFO, queue is FIFO.\n2. Stack uses push/pop; queue uses enqueue/dequeue.\n3. Stacks are used for recursion, queues for BFS.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Stack is LIFO, queue is FIFO.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Stack is LIFO, queue is FIFO.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
