    slug: "linkedlist-vs-array",
    shortDescription: "Compare linked list and array.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nArrays have contiguous memory, O(1) access, but fixed size (or amortized) and O(n) insert/delete in middle. Linked lists use dynamic memory, O(1) insertion/deletion at head, but O(n) access. Key points to remember: - Array: random access, cache-friendly, but resizing cost. - Linked list: no random access, extra pointer memory, but dynamic size and easy insertion/deletion. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a linked list and an array and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Arrays have contiguous memory, O(1) access, but fixed size (or amortized) and O(n) insert/delete in middle.\n2. Linked lists use dynamic memory, O(1) insertion/deletion at head, but O(n) access.\n3. Key points to remember: - Array: random access, cache-friendly, but resizing cost.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Arrays have contiguous memory, O(1) access, but fixed size (or amortized) and O(n) insert/delete in middle.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Arrays have contiguous memory, O(1) access, but fixed size (or amortized) and O(n) insert/delete in middle.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
