    question: "What is a circular queue and why is it used?",
    slug: "circular-queue",
    shortDescription: "Explain circular queue.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA circular queue connects the last position back to the first, allowing reuse of empty slots. It solves the problem of wasted space in a linear queue after dequeue operations. It uses head and tail pointers and modulo arithmetic. Key points to remember: - Enqueue: increment tail modulo size. - Dequeue: increment head modulo size. - Full condition: (tail+1)%size == head (if reserving one slot). - Used in buffering (e.g., circular buffers). A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a circular queue and why is it used accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A circular queue connects the last position back to the first, allowing reuse of empty slots.\n2. It solves the problem of wasted space in a linear queue after dequeue operations.\n3. It uses head and tail pointers and modulo arithmetic.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A circular queue connects the last position back to the first, allowing reuse of empty slots.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A circular queue connects the last position back to the first, allowing reuse of empty slots.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
