    question: "What is the difference between a B-tree and a B+ tree?",
    slug: "btree-vs-bplus",
    shortDescription: "Compare B-tree and B+ tree.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nB-tree and B+ tree are balanced tree structures for storage systems. B+ tree stores all keys in leaves linked sequentially, while B-tree stores keys in internal nodes too. B+ trees are better for range queries. Key points to remember: - B-tree: each node holds keys and data pointers; internal nodes also hold data. - B+ tree: internal nodes only hold keys for routing; all data is in leaves, which are linked for sequential access. - B+ tree: higher fanout, less height, efficient range scan. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a b-tree and a b+ tree and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. B-tree and B+ tree are balanced tree structures for storage systems.\n2. B+ tree stores all keys in leaves linked sequentially, while B-tree stores keys in internal nodes too.\n3. B+ trees are better for range queries.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. B-tree and B+ tree are balanced tree structures for storage systems.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. B-tree and B+ tree are balanced tree structures for storage systems.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nB-tree and B+ tree are balanced tree structures for storage systems. B+ tree stores all keys in leaves linked sequentially, while B-tree stores keys in internal nodes too. B+ trees are better for range queries. Key points to remember: - B-tree: each node holds keys and data pointers; internal nodes also hold data. - B+ tree: internal nodes only hold keys for routing; all data is in leaves, which are linked for sequential access. - B+ tree: higher fanout, less height, efficient range scan. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a b-tree and a b+ tree and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. B-tree and B+ tree are balanced tree structures for storage systems.\n2. B+ tree stores all keys in leaves linked sequentially, while B-tree stores keys in internal nodes too.\n3. B+ trees are better for range queries.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. B-tree and B+ tree are balanced tree structures for storage systems.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. B-tree and B+ tree are balanced tree structures for storage systems.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
