    slug: "skip-list",
    shortDescription: "Explain skip list.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA skip list is a probabilistic data structure that allows O(log n) average search, insertion, and deletion. It consists of multiple levels of linked lists, with higher levels skipping nodes. Simpler to implement than balanced trees. Key points to remember: - Each node has forward pointers at different levels. - Search: start from highest level, move forward until exceed key, then descend. - Insert: randomly choose level. A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a skip list accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A skip list is a probabilistic data structure that allows O(log n) average search, insertion, and deletion.\n2. It consists of multiple levels of linked lists, with higher levels skipping nodes.\n3. Simpler to implement than balanced trees.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A skip list is a probabilistic data structure that allows O(log n) average search, insertion, and deletion.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A skip list is a probabilistic data structure that allows O(log n) average search, insertion, and deletion.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
