    question: "What is a graph cycle and how to detect it?",
    slug: "graph-cycle-detection",
    shortDescription: "Explain cycle detection.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA cycle in a graph is a path where the start and end vertices are the same. In undirected graphs, DFS with parent detection; in directed graphs, DFS with recursion stack (back edges). Key points to remember: - Undirected: if during DFS we encounter a visited vertex that is not the parent, cycle. - Directed: maintain a recursion stack; if we see a vertex already in the stack, cycle. - Union-find can also detect cycles in undirected. A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a graph cycle and how to detect it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A cycle in a graph is a path where the start and end vertices are the same.\n2. In undirected graphs, DFS with parent detection; in directed graphs, DFS with recursion stack (back edges).\n3. Key points to remember: - Undirected: if during DFS we encounter a visited vertex that is not the parent, cycle.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A cycle in a graph is a path where the start and end vertices are the same.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A cycle in a graph is a path where the start and end vertices are the same.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
