    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nBinary search has O(log n) time complexity. It works on sorted arrays by repeatedly dividing the search interval in half. Key points to remember: - Compare target with middle element. - If equal, found. - If target less, search left half; else right half. - Each step halves the array, hence log n. For an interview, state the normal/average complexity first, then mention the worst case or important assumptions. Complexity depends on the implementation and the operation being measured, so always name those assumptions.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the time complexity of binary search accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Binary search has O(log n) time complexity.\n2. It works on sorted arrays by repeatedly dividing the search interval in half.\n3. Key points to remember: - Compare target with middle element.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Binary search has O(log n) time complexity.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Binary search has O(log n) time complexity.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
