    shortDescription: "Compare stable and unstable sort.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA stable sort maintains the relative order of equal elements; an unstable sort does not. Mergesort is stable; quicksort is typically unstable. Key points to remember: - Stability is important when sorting by multiple keys. - Example: sorting by first name then last name; stable ensures original order preserved for ties. For an interview, state the normal/average complexity first, then mention the worst case or important assumptions. Complexity depends on the implementation and the operation being measured, so always name those assumptions.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between stable and unstable sorting and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A stable sort maintains the relative order of equal elements; an unstable sort does not.\n2. Mergesort is stable; quicksort is typically unstable.\n3. Key points to remember: - Stability is important when sorting by multiple keys.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A stable sort maintains the relative order of equal elements; an unstable sort does not.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A stable sort maintains the relative order of equal elements; an unstable sort does not.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
