    slug: "dfs-vs-bfs",
    shortDescription: "Compare DFS and BFS.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDFS goes deep along a branch before backtracking; uses stack (recursion). BFS explores neighbors level by level; uses queue. DFS is memory-efficient for deep graphs; BFS finds shortest path in unweighted graphs. Key points to remember: - DFS: stack, time O(V+E), space O(V). - BFS: queue, time O(V+E), space O(V). - DFS for connectivity, topological sort; BFS for shortest paths. For an interview, state the normal/average complexity first, then mention the worst case or important assumptions. Complexity depends on the implementation and the operation being measured, so always name those assumptions.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between depth-first search (dfs) and breadth-first search (bfs) and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. DFS goes deep along a branch before backtracking; uses stack (recursion).\n2. BFS explores neighbors level by level; uses queue.\n3. DFS is memory-efficient for deep graphs; BFS finds shortest path in unweighted graphs.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. DFS goes deep along a branch before backtracking; uses stack (recursion).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. DFS goes deep along a branch before backtracking; uses stack (recursion).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
