    slug: "knapsack",
    shortDescription: "Explain knapsack.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe 0/1 knapsack problem: given items with weights and values, maximize total value without exceeding capacity. Solved with DP: dp[i][w] = max(dp[i-1][w], dp[i-1][w-wi] + vi). Time O(nW), space O(nW) or O(W). Key points to remember: - DP table: rows items, columns capacities. - Transition: either skip or take item. - Can be optimized to 1D array. A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the knapsack problem and its solution accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The 0/1 knapsack problem: given items with weights and values, maximize total value without exceeding capacity.\n2. Solved with DP: dp[i][w] = max(dp[i-1][w], dp[i-1][w-wi] + vi).\n3. Time O(nW), space O(nW) or O(W).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. The 0/1 knapsack problem: given items with weights and values, maximize total value without exceeding capacity.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. The 0/1 knapsack problem: given items with weights and values, maximize total value without exceeding capacity.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
