    slug: "lcs",
    shortDescription: "Explain LCS.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLCS finds the longest subsequence common to two strings. DP: if chars match, dp[i][j]=1+dp[i-1][j-1]; else max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]). Time O(mn). Key points to remember: - Build DP table. - Reconstruct sequence by backtracking. A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the longest common subsequence (LCS) problem accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. LCS finds the longest subsequence common to two strings.\n2. DP: if chars match, dp[i][j]=1+dp[i-1][j-1]; else max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]).\n3. Time O(mn).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. LCS finds the longest subsequence common to two strings.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. LCS finds the longest subsequence common to two strings.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
