    question: "What is the difference between greedy and dynamic programming?",
    slug: "greedy-vs-dp",
    shortDescription: "Compare greedy and DP.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nGreedy makes locally optimal choices hoping for global optimum; DP explores all possibilities and uses overlapping subproblems. Greedy is faster but not always optimal; DP is optimal but slower. Key points to remember: - Greedy: no backtracking, assumes optimal substructure and greedy choice property. - DP: solves subproblems and combines, optimal substructure and overlapping subproblems. - Examples: greedy: coin change (with certain denominations), DP: 0/1 knapsack. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between greedy and dynamic programming and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Greedy makes locally optimal choices hoping for global optimum; DP explores all possibilities and uses overlapping subproblems.\n2. Greedy is faster but not always optimal; DP is optimal but slower.\n3. Key points to remember: - Greedy: no backtracking, assumes optimal substructure and greedy choice property.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Greedy makes locally optimal choices hoping for global optimum; DP explores all possibilities and uses overlapping subproblems.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Greedy makes locally optimal choices hoping for global optimum; DP explores all possibilities and uses overlapping subproblems.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
