    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n- Quick sort: O(n log n) avg, O(n^2) worst. - Merge sort: O(n log n) all cases. - Heap sort: O(n log n) all cases. - Bubble/Selection/Insertion: O(n^2) worst. - Counting/Radix: O(n+k) or O(d(n+k)) for integer keys. Key points to remember: - Stable vs unstable also matters. - In-place vs not. For an interview, state the normal/average complexity first, then mention the worst case or important assumptions. Complexity depends on the implementation and the operation being measured, so always name those assumptions.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question is useful because summarize sorting complexities. The interviewer can then probe the mechanism, limitation, or practical consequence behind that answer.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. - Quick sort: O(n log n) avg, O(n^2) worst.\n2. - Merge sort: O(n log n) all cases.\n3. - Heap sort: O(n log n) all cases.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. - Quick sort: O(n log n) avg, O(n^2) worst.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. - Quick sort: O(n log n) avg, O(n^2) worst.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
