    question: "What is the difference between a divide-and-conquer and dynamic programming?",
    slug: "divide-conquer-vs-dp",
    shortDescription: "Compare paradigms.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nBoth break problems into subproblems. Divide-and-conquer solves independent subproblems recursively (e.g., mergesort); DP solves overlapping subproblems and stores results (e.g., Fibonacci). Key points to remember: - D&C: subproblems don't overlap, no memoization. - DP: overlapping subproblems, memoization or tabulation. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a divide-and-conquer and dynamic programming and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Both break problems into subproblems.\n2. Divide-and-conquer solves independent subproblems recursively (e.g., mergesort); DP solves overlapping subproblems and stores results (e.g., Fibonacci).\n3. Key points to remember: - D&C: subproblems don't overlap, no memoization.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Both break problems into subproblems.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Both break problems into subproblems.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
