    slug: "greedy-vs-backtracking",
    shortDescription: "Compare greedy and backtracking.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nGreedy makes a single pass, making locally optimal choices. Backtracking explores multiple possibilities, undoing choices when they fail. Greedy is fast but may not find optimal; backtracking finds optimal (exhaustive) but slow. Key points to remember: - Greedy: choice once, no undo. - Backtracking: choice then undo on failure. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a greedy algorithm and a backtracking algorithm and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Greedy makes a single pass, making locally optimal choices.\n2. Backtracking explores multiple possibilities, undoing choices when they fail.\n3. Greedy is fast but may not find optimal; backtracking finds optimal (exhaustive) but slow.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Greedy makes a single pass, making locally optimal choices.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Greedy makes a single pass, making locally optimal choices.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
