    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSharding is horizontal partitioning of data across multiple databases or nodes. Each shard holds a subset of data. Pros: scalability, performance. Cons: complexity, cross-shard queries, rebalancing. Key points to remember: - Shard key selection is critical (e.g., user ID). - Range sharding vs hash sharding. A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain database sharding and its pros/cons accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Sharding is horizontal partitioning of data across multiple databases or nodes.\n2. Each shard holds a subset of data.\n3. Pros: scalability, performance.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Sharding is horizontal partitioning of data across multiple databases or nodes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Sharding is horizontal partitioning of data across multiple databases or nodes.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
