    question: "What is the difference between vertical and horizontal scaling?",
    slug: "vertical-vs-horizontal-scaling",
    shortDescription: "Compare scaling methods.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nVertical scaling adds resources (CPU/RAM) to a single machine; horizontal scaling adds more machines. Vertical is limited by hardware; horizontal is more scalable and fault-tolerant. Key points to remember: - Vertical: simpler, no distributed complexity; but single point of failure. - Horizontal: distributed, requires load balancing, but can scale indefinitely. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between vertical and horizontal scaling and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Vertical scaling adds resources (CPU/RAM) to a single machine; horizontal scaling adds more machines.\n2. Vertical is limited by hardware; horizontal is more scalable and fault-tolerant.\n3. Key points to remember: - Vertical: simpler, no distributed complexity; but single point of failure.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Vertical scaling adds resources (CPU/RAM) to a single machine; horizontal scaling adds more machines.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Vertical scaling adds resources (CPU/RAM) to a single machine; horizontal scaling adds more machines.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
