    question: "What is the difference between SQL and NoSQL databases?",
    slug: "sql-vs-nosql",
    shortDescription: "Compare SQL and NoSQL.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSQL databases are relational systems that organize data into tables and provide SQL plus strong transactional and integrity features. NoSQL is a broad category that includes document, key-value, wide-column, and graph databases; their consistency, transaction, schema, and scaling characteristics vary by product. The right choice depends on access patterns, relationships, consistency requirements, transaction boundaries, scale, and operational needs rather than a blanket claim that one category is better. Key points to remember: - SQL: structured, join-intensive, vertical scaling. - NoSQL: unstructured, horizontal scaling, high availability. - Use cases: SQL for banking, NoSQL for social media. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between sql and nosql databases and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. SQL databases are relational systems that organize data into tables and provide SQL plus strong transactional and integrity features.\n2. NoSQL is a broad category that includes document, key-value, wide-column, and graph databases; their consistency, transaction, schema, and scaling characteristics vary by product.\n3. The right choice depends on access patterns, relationships, consistency requirements, transaction boundaries, scale, and operational needs rather than a blanket claim that one category is better.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, an engineer can use the concept in a concrete query or transaction and verify the resulting rows, locks, or execution plan. SQL databases are relational systems that organize data into tables and provide SQL plus strong transactional and integrity features.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. SQL databases are relational systems that organize data into tables and provide SQL plus strong transactional and integrity features.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
