    question: "What is the difference between monolithic and microservice architecture?",
    slug: "monolith-vs-microservices",
    shortDescription: "Compare architectures.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMonolith has a single codebase and deployment unit; microservices are split into multiple independent services. Monolith is simpler to develop initially, but harder to scale and maintain; microservices offer flexibility and resilience but add complexity. Key points to remember: - Monolith: single deployable artifact, simpler testing, but all changes affect whole system. - Microservices: independent services, technology heterogeneity, but distributed overhead. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between monolithic and microservice architecture and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Monolith has a single codebase and deployment unit; microservices are split into multiple independent services.\n2. Monolith is simpler to develop initially, but harder to scale and maintain; microservices offer flexibility and resilience but add complexity.\n3. Key points to remember: - Monolith: single deployable artifact, simpler testing, but all changes affect whole system.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Monolith has a single codebase and deployment unit; microservices are split into multiple independent services.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Monolith has a single codebase and deployment unit; microservices are split into multiple independent services.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMonolith has a single codebase and deployment unit; microservices are split into multiple independent services. Monolith is simpler to develop initially, but harder to scale and maintain; microservices offer flexibility and resilience but add complexity. Key points to remember: - Monolith: single deployable artifact, simpler testing, but all changes affect whole system. - Microservices: independent services, technology heterogeneity, but distributed overhead. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between monolithic and microservice architecture and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Monolith has a single codebase and deployment unit; microservices are split into multiple independent services.\n2. Monolith is simpler to develop initially, but harder to scale and maintain; microservices offer flexibility and resilience but add complexity.\n3. Key points to remember: - Monolith: single deployable artifact, simpler testing, but all changes affect whole system.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Monolith has a single codebase and deployment unit; microservices are split into multiple independent services.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Monolith has a single codebase and deployment unit; microservices are split into multiple independent services.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
