    question: "What is an API gateway?",
    slug: "api-gateway",
    shortDescription: "Explain API gateway.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn API gateway is a single entry point for all client requests in a microservices architecture. It handles routing, authentication, rate limiting, caching, and logging. It shields clients from service internals. Key points to remember: - Routes requests to appropriate services. - May aggregate responses. - Implements cross-cutting concerns. A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain an API gateway accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. An API gateway is a single entry point for all client requests in a microservices architecture.\n2. It handles routing, authentication, rate limiting, caching, and logging.\n3. It shields clients from service internals.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. An API gateway is a single entry point for all client requests in a microservices architecture.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. An API gateway is a single entry point for all client requests in a microservices architecture.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
