    question: "What is the difference between stateful and stateless services?",
    slug: "stateful-vs-stateless",
    shortDescription: "Compare stateful and stateless.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nStateful services maintain client state (e.g., session) across requests; stateless services do not. Stateless are easier to scale horizontally; stateful require sticky sessions or shared storage. Key points to remember: - Stateless: each request can be handled by any instance, no dependency on previous requests. Easier to scale. - Stateful: need to keep session data; can be handled by external storage (Redis) or sticky sessions. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between stateful and stateless services and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Stateful services maintain client state (e.g., session) across requests; stateless services do not.\n2. Stateless are easier to scale horizontally; stateful require sticky sessions or shared storage.\n3. Key points to remember: - Stateless: each request can be handled by any instance, no dependency on previous requests.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Stateful services maintain client state (e.g., session) across requests; stateless services do not.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Stateful services maintain client state (e.g., session) across requests; stateless services do not.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
