    question: "What is the difference between a forward proxy and a reverse proxy?",
    slug: "forward-vs-reverse-proxy",
    shortDescription: "Compare proxies.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA forward proxy sits between client and internet, forwarding requests on behalf of clients (e.g., for anonymity). A reverse proxy sits between internet and servers, forwarding requests to backend servers (e.g., for load balancing, caching). Key points to remember: - Forward proxy: client-side, hides client identity. - Reverse proxy: server-side, hides server identity, provides load balancing, SSL termination. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a forward proxy and a reverse proxy and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A forward proxy sits between client and internet, forwarding requests on behalf of clients (e.g., for anonymity).\n2. A reverse proxy sits between internet and servers, forwarding requests to backend servers (e.g., for load balancing, caching).\n3. Key points to remember: - Forward proxy: client-side, hides client identity.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A forward proxy sits between client and internet, forwarding requests on behalf of clients (e.g., for anonymity).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A forward proxy sits between client and internet, forwarding requests on behalf of clients (e.g., for anonymity).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
