    question: "What is the difference between a database and a data warehouse?",
    slug: "db-vs-data-warehouse",
    shortDescription: "Compare database and data warehouse.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA database is an OLTP system for transactional processing (CRUD). A data warehouse is an OLAP system for analytics and reporting, storing historical data from multiple sources. Key points to remember: - Database: normalized, row-oriented, fast writes. - Data warehouse: denormalized, columnar storage, optimized for read-heavy queries. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a database and a data warehouse and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A database is an OLTP system for transactional processing (CRUD).\n2. A data warehouse is an OLAP system for analytics and reporting, storing historical data from multiple sources.\n3. Key points to remember: - Database: normalized, row-oriented, fast writes.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A database is an OLTP system for transactional processing (CRUD).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A database is an OLTP system for transactional processing (CRUD).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
