    shortDescription: "Compare abstraction and encapsulation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAbstraction focuses on what an object does (interface); encapsulation focuses on how it does it (implementation details). Abstraction hides complexity; encapsulation hides data. Key points to remember: - Abstraction: using abstract classes/interfaces to define contracts. - Encapsulation: using private fields and public getters/setters. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between abstraction and encapsulation and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Abstraction focuses on what an object does (interface); encapsulation focuses on how it does it (implementation details).\n2. Abstraction hides complexity; encapsulation hides data.\n3. Key points to remember: - Abstraction: using abstract classes/interfaces to define contracts.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Abstraction focuses on what an object does (interface); encapsulation focuses on how it does it (implementation details).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Abstraction focuses on what an object does (interface); encapsulation focuses on how it does it (implementation details).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
