    question: "What is polymorphism and its types?",
    slug: "polymorphism-types",
    shortDescription: "Explain polymorphism.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPolymorphism means 'many forms'. Compile-time (method overloading) and runtime (method overriding). Runtime polymorphism uses dynamic binding to call the correct method based on object type. Key points to remember: - Overloading: same method name, different parameters (compile-time). - Overriding: subclass provides specific implementation (runtime). A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain polymorphism and its types accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Polymorphism means 'many forms'.\n2. Compile-time (method overloading) and runtime (method overriding).\n3. Runtime polymorphism uses dynamic binding to call the correct method based on object type.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Polymorphism means 'many forms'.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Polymorphism means 'many forms'.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
