    focus: null,
    question: "What is the difference between overloading and overriding?",
    slug: "overloading-vs-overriding",
    shortDescription: "Compare overloading and overriding.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nOverloading occurs within the same class (or subclass) with different parameters; overriding occurs in a subclass to replace a parent method with the same signature. Overloading is compile-time; overriding is runtime. Key points to remember: - Overloading: method name same, parameters differ; return type may differ. - Overriding: method signature identical; must have same return type (covariant allowed); cannot reduce access. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between overloading and overriding and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Overloading occurs within the same class (or subclass) with different parameters; overriding occurs in a subclass to replace a parent method with the same signature.\n2. Overloading is compile-time; overriding is runtime.\n3. Key points to remember: - Overloading: method name same, parameters differ; return type may differ.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Overloading occurs within the same class (or subclass) with different parameters; overriding occurs in a subclass to replace a parent method with the same signature.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Overloading occurs within the same class (or subclass) with different parameters; overriding occurs in a subclass to replace a parent method with the same signature.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
