    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nEncapsulation bundles data (fields) and methods that operate on that data into a single unit (class), and hides internal state using access modifiers (private, protected). It is achieved by making fields private and providing public getters/setters. Key points to remember: - Protects data integrity. - Allows controlled access via methods. - Reduces coupling. A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain encapsulation and how to achieve it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Encapsulation bundles data (fields) and methods that operate on that data into a single unit (class), and hides internal state using access modifiers (private, protected).\n2. It is achieved by making fields private and providing public getters/setters.\n3. Key points to remember: - Protects data integrity.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Encapsulation bundles data (fields) and methods that operate on that data into a single unit (class), and hides internal state using access modifiers (private, protected).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Encapsulation bundles data (fields) and methods that operate on that data into a single unit (class), and hides internal state using access modifiers (private, protected).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
