    slug: "isolation-levels",
    shortDescription: "Explain isolation levels.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIsolation levels define the degree to which transactions are isolated from each other. Read Uncommitted (dirty reads), Read Committed (no dirty reads), Repeatable Read (consistent reads), Serializable (strongest). Key points to remember: - Read Uncommitted: allows dirty reads. - Read Committed: prevents dirty reads but allows non-repeatable reads. - Repeatable Read: ensures same row read same within transaction. - Serializable: prevents all anomalies but reduces concurrency. A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Isolation levels define the degree to which transactions are isolated from each other.\n2. Read Uncommitted (dirty reads), Read Committed (no dirty reads), Repeatable Read (consistent reads), Serializable (strongest).\n3. Key points to remember: - Read Uncommitted: allows dirty reads.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the requested list directly, then go deeper on the item most relevant to the interviewer’s context.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, an engineer can use the concept in a concrete query or transaction and verify the resulting rows, locks, or execution plan. Isolation levels define the degree to which transactions are isolated from each other.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Isolation levels define the degree to which transactions are isolated from each other.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
