    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nJoins combine rows from two or more tables based on related columns. Types: INNER JOIN, LEFT JOIN, RIGHT JOIN, FULL JOIN, and CROSS JOIN. Key points to remember: - INNER: returns only matching rows. - LEFT: all from left, matching from right. - RIGHT: all from right, matching from left. - FULL: all rows from both, filling nulls. A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a SQL join and its types accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Joins combine rows from two or more tables based on related columns.\n2. Types: INNER JOIN, LEFT JOIN, RIGHT JOIN, FULL JOIN, and CROSS JOIN.\n3. Key points to remember: - INNER: returns only matching rows.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, an engineer can use the concept in a concrete query or transaction and verify the resulting rows, locks, or execution plan. Joins combine rows from two or more tables based on related columns.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Joins combine rows from two or more tables based on related columns.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
