    slug: "stored-procedure",
    shortDescription: "Explain stored procedure.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA stored procedure is a named program defined and executed by a database system. It can accept parameters and contain SQL plus database-specific procedural logic. Stored procedures can centralize data-side operations, enforce controlled access patterns, and reduce application/database round trips in some workloads, but they can also increase vendor coupling and require careful versioning, testing, and performance management. Do not assume that every database precompiles procedures in a way that automatically makes them faster. Key points to remember: - Can accept parameters. - Can contain logic (loops, conditions). - Cons: vendor-specific, harder to version. A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks engineering judgment around what is a stored procedure and its advantages. A useful answer makes the benefit, cost, and condition for choosing the approach explicit.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A stored procedure is a named program defined and executed by a database system.\n2. It can accept parameters and contain SQL plus database-specific procedural logic.\n3. Stored procedures can centralize data-side operations, enforce controlled access patterns, and reduce application/database round trips in some workloads, but they can also increase vendor coupling and require careful versioning, testing, and performance management.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nState the benefit, the cost, and the constraint that decides whether the trade-off is worth accepting.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A stored procedure is a named program defined and executed by a database system.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A stored procedure is a named program defined and executed by a database system.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Listing benefits and drawbacks without saying when they matter.\n- Treating one approach as universally superior.\n- Ignoring operational cost, maintenance, or failure behavior.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nName the constraint that makes the trade-off acceptable, then state when you would deliberately choose another approach.",
