    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`UNION` combines results from two queries and removes duplicates; `UNION ALL` does not remove duplicates, so it's faster. Key points to remember: - UNION: performs distinct sort; overhead. - UNION ALL: simply concatenates; use when duplicates are acceptable or not present. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `union` and `union all` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `UNION` combines results from two queries and removes duplicates; `UNION ALL` does not remove duplicates, so it's faster.\n2. Key points to remember: - UNION: performs distinct sort; overhead.\n3. - UNION ALL: simply concatenates; use when duplicates are acceptable or not present.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `UNION` combines results from two queries and removes duplicates; `UNION ALL` does not remove duplicates, so it's faster.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `UNION` combines results from two queries and removes duplicates; `UNION ALL` does not remove duplicates, so it's faster.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
