    shortDescription: "Explain view.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA view is a virtual table based on the result of a SELECT query. It does not store data physically; it simplifies complex queries and provides security by restricting access to certain rows/columns. Key points to remember: - Can join multiple tables. - Can be updatable (under certain conditions). - Can be used to enforce row-level security. A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a view in SQL accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A view is a virtual table based on the result of a SELECT query.\n2. It does not store data physically; it simplifies complex queries and provides security by restricting access to certain rows/columns.\n3. Key points to remember: - Can join multiple tables.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, an engineer can use the concept in a concrete query or transaction and verify the resulting rows, locks, or execution plan. A view is a virtual table based on the result of a SELECT query.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A view is a virtual table based on the result of a SELECT query.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
