    slug: "osi-model",
    shortDescription: "Explain OSI.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe OSI model has 7 layers: Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application. It standardizes network communication. Key points to remember: - Physical: bits, cables. - Data Link: frames, MAC addresses. - Network: packets, IP addresses (routing). - Transport: segments, TCP/UDP (end-to-end). - Session: establishes, manages, and terminates connections. - Presentation: data translation (encryption, compression). - Application: user-facing protocols (HTTP, FTP). A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the OSI model and its layers accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The OSI model has 7 layers: Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application.\n2. It standardizes network communication.\n3. Key points to remember: - Physical: bits, cables.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. The OSI model has 7 layers: Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. The OSI model has 7 layers: Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
