    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTCP is connection-oriented, reliable, ordered, and has flow control. UDP is connectionless, unreliable, unordered, and has lower overhead. TCP for file transfer, UDP for streaming/real-time. Key points to remember: - TCP: three-way handshake, retransmission, congestion control. - UDP: no handshake, no retransmission, faster but lossy. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between tcp and udp and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. TCP is connection-oriented, reliable, ordered, and has flow control.\n2. UDP is connectionless, unreliable, unordered, and has lower overhead.\n3. TCP for file transfer, UDP for streaming/real-time.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. TCP is connection-oriented, reliable, ordered, and has flow control.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. TCP is connection-oriented, reliable, ordered, and has flow control.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
