    slug: "http-methods",
    shortDescription: "Explain HTTP.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) is an application-layer protocol for distributed, collaborative, hypermedia systems. Common methods: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH, HEAD, OPTIONS. Key points to remember: - GET: retrieve resource. - POST: create resource. - PUT: replace resource. - DELETE: remove resource. - PATCH: partial update. A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain HTTP and its methods accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) is an application-layer protocol for distributed, collaborative, hypermedia systems.\n2. Common methods: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH, HEAD, OPTIONS.\n3. Key points to remember: - GET: retrieve resource.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) is an application-layer protocol for distributed, collaborative, hypermedia systems.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) is an application-layer protocol for distributed, collaborative, hypermedia systems.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
