    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHTTPS is HTTP over TLS/SSL, providing encryption, integrity, and authentication. It prevents eavesdropping and man-in-the-middle attacks. Port 443 vs 80. Key points to remember: - HTTP: plaintext. - HTTPS: encrypted, uses certificates. - HTTPS is now standard for all sites. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between http and https and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. HTTPS is HTTP over TLS/SSL, providing encryption, integrity, and authentication.\n2. It prevents eavesdropping and man-in-the-middle attacks.\n3. Port 443 vs 80.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. HTTPS is HTTP over TLS/SSL, providing encryption, integrity, and authentication.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. HTTPS is HTTP over TLS/SSL, providing encryption, integrity, and authentication.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
