    question: "What is virtual memory and why is it used?",
    slug: "virtual-memory",
    shortDescription: "Explain virtual memory.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nVirtual memory allows a process to use more memory than physically available by using disk as backup. It provides isolation, simplifies programming, and enables paging/segmentation. Key points to remember: - Uses demand paging. - Page faults: when a page is not in RAM, load from disk. - Enables each process to have its own address space. A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain virtual memory and why is it used accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Virtual memory allows a process to use more memory than physically available by using disk as backup.\n2. It provides isolation, simplifies programming, and enables paging/segmentation.\n3. Key points to remember: - Uses demand paging.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Virtual memory allows a process to use more memory than physically available by using disk as backup.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Virtual memory allows a process to use more memory than physically available by using disk as backup.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
