    question: "What is the difference between multithreading and multiprocessing?",
    slug: "multithreading-vs-multiprocessing",
    shortDescription: "Compare multithreading and multiprocessing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMultithreading: multiple threads within a single process sharing memory. Multiprocessing: multiple processes running on multiple CPUs. Threads are lighter, but a crash in one thread can affect others; processes are isolated. Key points to remember: - Multithreading: concurrency within a process. - Multiprocessing: parallel execution on multiple cores. The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between multithreading and multiprocessing and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Multithreading: multiple threads within a single process sharing memory.\n2. Multiprocessing: multiple processes running on multiple CPUs.\n3. Threads are lighter, but a crash in one thread can affect others; processes are isolated.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Multithreading: multiple threads within a single process sharing memory.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Multithreading: multiple threads within a single process sharing memory.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
