    question: "What is the difference between a kernel and a shell?",
    slug: "kernel-vs-shell",
    shortDescription: "Compare kernel and shell.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe kernel is the core of the OS, managing hardware and system resources. The shell is a user interface (CLI) that interprets commands and interacts with the kernel. Key points to remember: - Kernel: runs in privileged mode, handles interrupts, memory, processes. - Shell: runs in user mode, provides user interaction (bash, zsh). The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a kernel and a shell and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The kernel is the core of the OS, managing hardware and system resources.\n2. The shell is a user interface (CLI) that interprets commands and interacts with the kernel.\n3. Key points to remember: - Kernel: runs in privileged mode, handles interrupts, memory, processes.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. The kernel is the core of the OS, managing hardware and system resources.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. The kernel is the core of the OS, managing hardware and system resources.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
