    shortDescription: "Explain YAGNI.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nYAGNI (You Aren't Gonna Need It) is a principle from Extreme Programming that advises against adding functionality until it is actually needed. It prevents wasted effort on speculative features. Key points to remember: - Focus on current requirements. - Refactor when needed; don't add features 'just in case'. A strong interview answer should connect the definition to how the concept works internally, its important trade-offs, and one realistic use case. The goal is not only to name the concept but to explain why and when it is useful.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain YAGNI accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. YAGNI (You Aren't Gonna Need It) is a principle from Extreme Programming that advises against adding functionality until it is actually needed.\n2. It prevents wasted effort on speculative features.\n3. Key points to remember: - Focus on current requirements.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. YAGNI (You Aren't Gonna Need It) is a principle from Extreme Programming that advises against adding functionality until it is actually needed.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. YAGNI (You Aren't Gonna Need It) is a principle from Extreme Programming that advises against adding functionality until it is actually needed.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
