    question: "What is the difference between a framework and a library?",
    slug: "framework-vs-library",
    shortDescription: "Compare framework and library.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA library is a collection of functions you call; it provides specific functionality. A framework is a structure that calls your code; it dictates the flow of control (inversion of control). Libraries are more flexible; frameworks provide more structure. Key points to remember: - Library: you choose when to use it. - Framework: you plug into it, it controls the flow (e.g., Spring). The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a framework and a library and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A library is a collection of functions you call; it provides specific functionality.\n2. A framework is a structure that calls your code; it dictates the flow of control (inversion of control).\n3. Libraries are more flexible; frameworks provide more structure.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A library is a collection of functions you call; it provides specific functionality.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A library is a collection of functions you call; it provides specific functionality.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
