    focus: null,
    question: "What is the difference between agile and waterfall?",
    slug: "agile-vs-waterfall",
    shortDescription: "Compare Agile and Waterfall.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWaterfall is a linear, sequential model; each phase completes before the next. Agile is iterative, incremental, and adaptive; it embraces change and delivers value frequently. Key points to remember: - Waterfall: rigid, long feedback cycles. - Agile: flexible, short cycles (sprints). The important point is that neither option is universally better. The correct choice depends on the required behavior, performance characteristics, complexity, and operational constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between agile and waterfall and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Waterfall is a linear, sequential model; each phase completes before the next.\n2. Agile is iterative, incremental, and adaptive; it embraces change and delivers value frequently.\n3. Key points to remember: - Waterfall: rigid, long feedback cycles.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Waterfall is a linear, sequential model; each phase completes before the next.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Waterfall is a linear, sequential model; each phase completes before the next.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
