    shortDescription: "Explain `@RestController`.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`@RestController` is a convenience annotation that combines `@Controller` and `@ResponseBody`. It indicates that the class is a controller where every method returns a domain object instead of a view (RESTful).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `@RestController` annotation accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `@RestController` is a convenience annotation that combines `@Controller` and `@ResponseBody`.\n2. It indicates that the class is a controller where every method returns a domain object instead of a view (RESTful).\n3. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Spring Boot service, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `@RestController` is a convenience annotation that combines `@Controller` and `@ResponseBody`.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Spring Boot service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `@RestController` is a convenience annotation that combines `@Controller` and `@ResponseBody`.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
