    question: "What is the difference between `@Component` and `@Bean`?",
    slug: "component-vs-bean",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`@Component` is used on a class and relies on classpath scanning to create beans. `@Bean` is used on a method in a `@Configuration` class and gives fine-grained control over bean creation. `@Bean` is typically used for third-party classes that you can't annotate.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `@component` and `@bean` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `@Component` is used on a class and relies on classpath scanning to create beans.\n2. `@Bean` is used on a method in a `@Configuration` class and gives fine-grained control over bean creation.\n3. `@Bean` is typically used for third-party classes that you can't annotate.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Spring Boot service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `@Component` is used on a class and relies on classpath scanning to create beans.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Spring Boot service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `@Component` is used on a class and relies on classpath scanning to create beans.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
