    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSingleton (default): one instance per container. Prototype: new instance each request. Request: one per HTTP request (web). Session: one per HTTP session. Application: one per servlet context. Websocket: one per websocket.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Singleton (default): one instance per container.\n2. Prototype: new instance each request.\n3. Request: one per HTTP request (web).\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Spring Boot service, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Singleton (default): one instance per container.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an application built with Spring Boot, what are the scopes of spring beans can affect runtime behavior or maintainability. Check the language/runtime or framework version before relying on a version-sensitive detail.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
