    slug: "onetomany-vs-manytoone",
    shortDescription: "Compare relationship annotations.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`@OneToMany` indicates a one-to-many relationship (parent to children). `@ManyToOne` indicates a many-to-one relationship (child to parent). They are opposites of the same relationship.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `@onetomany` and `@manytoone` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `@OneToMany` indicates a one-to-many relationship (parent to children).\n2. `@ManyToOne` indicates a many-to-one relationship (child to parent).\n3. They are opposites of the same relationship.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Spring Boot service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `@OneToMany` indicates a one-to-many relationship (parent to children).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Spring Boot service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `@OneToMany` indicates a one-to-many relationship (parent to children).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
