    shortDescription: "Explain pagination.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse `Pageable` and `Page<T>` in repository methods. Example: `Page<User> findAll(Pageable pageable)`. Also `Sort` for sorting. Controllers accept `PageRequest`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you implement pagination and sorting, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use `Pageable` and `Page<T>` in repository methods.\n2. Example: `Page<User> findAll(Pageable pageable)`.\n3. Also `Sort` for sorting.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a Spring Boot service can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Use `Pageable` and `Page<T>` in repository methods.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Spring Boot service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Use `Pageable` and `Page<T>` in repository methods.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
