    shortDescription: "Compare JPA and JDBC.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nJDBC is a low-level API for executing SQL statements and handling result sets. JPA is a higher-level ORM that maps Java objects to database tables, reducing boilerplate and providing persistence features.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between jpa and jdbc and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. JDBC is a low-level API for executing SQL statements and handling result sets.\n2. JPA is a higher-level ORM that maps Java objects to database tables, reducing boilerplate and providing persistence features.\n3. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Spring Boot service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. JDBC is a low-level API for executing SQL statements and handling result sets.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Spring Boot service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. JDBC is a low-level API for executing SQL statements and handling result sets.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
