    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`@Endpoint` is a generic endpoint for all technologies (JMX, web). `@WebEndpoint` is specifically for web exposure. Both are used to create custom Actuator endpoints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `@endpoint` and `@webendpoint` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `@Endpoint` is a generic endpoint for all technologies (JMX, web).\n2. `@WebEndpoint` is specifically for web exposure.\n3. Both are used to create custom Actuator endpoints.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Spring Boot service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `@Endpoint` is a generic endpoint for all technologies (JMX, web).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Spring Boot service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `@Endpoint` is a generic endpoint for all technologies (JMX, web).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
