    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nJAR is self-contained with embedded server; WAR is deployed to an external servlet container (Tomcat, Jetty). JAR is simpler and recommended for modern microservices.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between jar and war deployment and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. JAR is self-contained with embedded server; WAR is deployed to an external servlet container (Tomcat, Jetty).\n2. JAR is simpler and recommended for modern microservices.\n3. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Spring Boot service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. JAR is self-contained with embedded server; WAR is deployed to an external servlet container (Tomcat, Jetty).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Spring Boot service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. JAR is self-contained with embedded server; WAR is deployed to an external servlet container (Tomcat, Jetty).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
