    slug: "mysql-overview",
    shortDescription: "Define MySQL and list its key features.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMySQL is an open-source relational database management system (RDBMS) that uses SQL. It is widely used for transactional applications and supports multiple storage engines, with InnoDB as the default in MySQL 8.4. Important capabilities include ACID transactions through InnoDB, foreign keys, indexes, replication, full-text search, JSON data, stored programs, and a broad SQL feature set.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain MySQL and what are its main features accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. MySQL is an open-source relational database management system (RDBMS) that uses SQL.\n2. It is widely used for transactional applications and supports multiple storage engines, with InnoDB as the default in MySQL 8.4.\n3. Important capabilities include ACID transactions through InnoDB, foreign keys, indexes, replication, full-text search, JSON data, stored programs, and a broad SQL feature set.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, an engineer can use the concept in a concrete query or transaction and verify the resulting rows, locks, or execution plan. MySQL is an open-source relational database management system (RDBMS) that uses SQL.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. MySQL is an open-source relational database management system (RDBMS) that uses SQL.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
