    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDELETE is DML and can remove selected rows with WHERE; for transactional InnoDB tables it can be rolled back before COMMIT. TRUNCATE TABLE removes all rows and is DDL-like in MySQL, causes an implicit commit, and cannot be rolled back as a normal transactional DML operation. TRUNCATE is generally faster for removing the whole table contents and resets the AUTO_INCREMENT counter.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `delete` and `truncate` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. DELETE is DML and can remove selected rows with WHERE; for transactional InnoDB tables it can be rolled back before COMMIT.\n2. TRUNCATE TABLE removes all rows and is DDL-like in MySQL, causes an implicit commit, and cannot be rolled back as a normal transactional DML operation.\n3. TRUNCATE is generally faster for removing the whole table contents and resets the AUTO_INCREMENT counter.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. DELETE is DML and can remove selected rows with WHERE; for transactional InnoDB tables it can be rolled back before COMMIT.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. DELETE is DML and can remove selected rows with WHERE; for transactional InnoDB tables it can be rolled back before COMMIT.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDELETE is DML and can remove selected rows with WHERE; for transactional InnoDB tables it can be rolled back before COMMIT. TRUNCATE TABLE removes all rows and is DDL-like in MySQL, causes an implicit commit, and cannot be rolled back as a normal transactional DML operation. TRUNCATE is generally faster for removing the whole table contents and resets the AUTO_INCREMENT counter.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `delete` and `truncate` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. DELETE is DML and can remove selected rows with WHERE; for transactional InnoDB tables it can be rolled back before COMMIT.\n2. TRUNCATE TABLE removes all rows and is DDL-like in MySQL, causes an implicit commit, and cannot be rolled back as a normal transactional DML operation.\n3. TRUNCATE is generally faster for removing the whole table contents and resets the AUTO_INCREMENT counter.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. DELETE is DML and can remove selected rows with WHERE; for transactional InnoDB tables it can be rolled back before COMMIT.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. DELETE is DML and can remove selected rows with WHERE; for transactional InnoDB tables it can be rolled back before COMMIT.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
