    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`SELECT. FOR SHARE` is the modern syntax for a locking read that takes shared locks on qualifying InnoDB records; `LOCK IN SHARE MODE` is the older equivalent. Other transactions can generally read the locked records, but conflicting updates are blocked until the transaction ends. Use it only when a consistent, protected read is actually required.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a `SELECT ... LOCK IN SHARE MODE` accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `SELECT.\n2. FOR SHARE` is the modern syntax for a locking read that takes shared locks on qualifying InnoDB records; `LOCK IN SHARE MODE` is the older equivalent.\n3. Other transactions can generally read the locked records, but conflicting updates are blocked until the transaction ends.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `SELECT.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `SELECT.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
