    shortDescription: "List the types.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCommon MySQL index forms include PRIMARY KEY indexes, UNIQUE indexes, ordinary secondary indexes, FULLTEXT indexes, and SPATIAL indexes. InnoDB organizes its primary-key data in the clustered index; secondary indexes contain the primary-key value needed to locate the clustered record. The exact behavior and supported index features depend on the storage engine and data type.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Common MySQL index forms include PRIMARY KEY indexes, UNIQUE indexes, ordinary secondary indexes, FULLTEXT indexes, and SPATIAL indexes.\n2. InnoDB organizes its primary-key data in the clustered index; secondary indexes contain the primary-key value needed to locate the clustered record.\n3. The exact behavior and supported index features depend on the storage engine and data type.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the requested list directly, then go deeper on the item most relevant to the interviewer’s context.\n\n**Example :**\nFor `SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = ? ORDER BY created_at DESC`, an index such as `(customer_id, created_at)` may help, but the actual benefit should be confirmed from the query plan and data distribution.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a data-backed service, what are the different types of indexes in mysql matters when correctness, access patterns, concurrency, and query or storage cost have to work together. Measure the real workload rather than assuming a design choice is always beneficial.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
