    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA composite index is an index on multiple columns. The order of columns matters: queries that filter on the leftmost prefix can use the index. Example: `INDEX(last_name, first_name)` supports searches on `last_name` and both.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a composite index (multi-column index) accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A composite index is an index on multiple columns.\n2. The order of columns matters: queries that filter on the leftmost prefix can use the index.\n3. Example: `INDEX(last_name, first_name)` supports searches on `last_name` and both.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor `SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = ? ORDER BY created_at DESC`, an index such as `(customer_id, created_at)` may help, but the actual benefit should be confirmed from the query plan and data distribution.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A composite index is an index on multiple columns.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
