    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA `UNIQUE` index ensures that no two rows have the same value in the indexed column(s). It also speeds up lookups. Use it for alternate keys (e.g., email).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer wants the reasoning behind when would you use a `unique` index, not just the definition. Explain the problem it solves and the condition that would make you choose another approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A `UNIQUE` index ensures that no two rows have the same value in the indexed column(s).\n2. It also speeds up lookups.\n3. Use it for alternate keys (e.g., email).\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nName the constraint first, explain why this approach fits it, and finish with the main case where it does not fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor `SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = ? ORDER BY created_at DESC`, an index such as `(customer_id, created_at)` may help, but the actual benefit should be confirmed from the query plan and data distribution.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a data-backed service, when would you use a `unique` index matters when correctness, access patterns, concurrency, and query or storage cost have to work together. Measure the real workload rather than assuming a design choice is always beneficial.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving a definition instead of answering the reason or decision.\n- Stating an absolute rule without assumptions.\n- Missing the condition that would make another approach preferable.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTie the reason to a concrete requirement or failure mode. A “why” answer is stronger when it explains what would make the recommendation change.",
