    shortDescription: "Explain table maintenance.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`OPTIMIZE TABLE` reorganizes the physical storage of a table and its indexes to reclaim unused space and defragment. It may use table-locking (InnoDB supports online DDL).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `OPTIMIZE TABLE` command accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `OPTIMIZE TABLE` reorganizes the physical storage of a table and its indexes to reclaim unused space and defragment.\n2. It may use table-locking (InnoDB supports online DDL).\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `OPTIMIZE TABLE` reorganizes the physical storage of a table and its indexes to reclaim unused space and defragment.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `OPTIMIZE TABLE` reorganizes the physical storage of a table and its indexes to reclaim unused space and defragment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
