    slug: "mysql-transaction",
    shortDescription: "Define ACID properties.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA transaction groups related database operations into an atomic unit. With InnoDB, transactions provide ACID properties: atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability. Typical control statements are `START TRANSACTION`, `COMMIT`, and `ROLLBACK`. A good transaction keeps related changes together and commits only after all required work succeeds.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a transaction in MySQL accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A transaction groups related database operations into an atomic unit.\n2. With InnoDB, transactions provide ACID properties: atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability.\n3. Typical control statements are `START TRANSACTION`, `COMMIT`, and `ROLLBACK`.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nPerform the debit and credit inside one transaction and commit only after both statements succeed; on failure, roll back the transaction.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A transaction groups related database operations into an atomic unit.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
