    slug: "read-committed-vs-repeatable-read",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`READ COMMITTED` allows non-repeatable reads (different results in the same transaction). `REPEATABLE READ` guarantees that within a transaction, repeated reads return the same data (using MVCC snapshots).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `read committed` and `repeatable read` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `READ COMMITTED` allows non-repeatable reads (different results in the same transaction).\n2. `REPEATABLE READ` guarantees that within a transaction, repeated reads return the same data (using MVCC snapshots).\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `READ COMMITTED` allows non-repeatable reads (different results in the same transaction).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `READ COMMITTED` allows non-repeatable reads (different results in the same transaction).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
