    slug: "single-transaction",
    shortDescription: "Explain its importance.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`mysqldump --single-transaction` starts a transaction to obtain a consistent snapshot for transactional tables such as InnoDB without taking a global table lock for the dump. It is useful for reducing application disruption during logical backups. It does not make nontransactional tables such as MyISAM transactionally consistent, and concurrent DDL can still affect the dump.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `--single-transaction` option in mysqldump accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `mysqldump --single-transaction` starts a transaction to obtain a consistent snapshot for transactional tables such as InnoDB without taking a global table lock for the dump.\n2. It is useful for reducing application disruption during logical backups.\n3. It does not make nontransactional tables such as MyISAM transactionally consistent, and concurrent DDL can still affect the dump.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nPerform the debit and credit inside one transaction and commit only after both statements succeed; on failure, roll back the transaction.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `mysqldump --single-transaction` starts a transaction to obtain a consistent snapshot for transactional tables such as InnoDB without taking a global table lock for the dump.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
