    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nXA transactions allow multiple databases to participate in a single distributed transaction, using a two-phase commit protocol. MySQL supports XA with InnoDB.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain `XA` transactions accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. XA transactions allow multiple databases to participate in a single distributed transaction, using a two-phase commit protocol.\n2. MySQL supports XA with InnoDB.\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nPerform the debit and credit inside one transaction and commit only after both statements succeed; on failure, roll back the transaction.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. XA transactions allow multiple databases to participate in a single distributed transaction, using a two-phase commit protocol.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
