    shortDescription: "List scalability attributes.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA scalable system handles increasing load gracefully. Key characteristics: horizontal scaling (adding more machines), vertical scaling (more powerful machines), load balancing, statelessness, efficient caching, database partitioning, and asynchronous processing.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A scalable system handles increasing load gracefully.\n2. Key characteristics: horizontal scaling (adding more machines), vertical scaling (more powerful machines), load balancing, statelessness, efficient caching, database partitioning, and asynchronous processing.\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A scalable system handles increasing load gracefully.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production platform, what are the key characteristics of a scalable system affects component boundaries, capacity, latency, consistency, or failure handling. The design should start from traffic and reliability assumptions, then choose the simplest architecture that meets them.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
