    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPerformance is about speed: how quickly a system responds to a single request. Scalability is about handling growth: how the system performs under increasing load. A system can be fast but not scalable, and vice versa.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between scalability and performance and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Performance is about speed: how quickly a system responds to a single request.\n2. Scalability is about handling growth: how the system performs under increasing load.\n3. A system can be fast but not scalable, and vice versa.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Performance is about speed: how quickly a system responds to a single request.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Performance is about speed: how quickly a system responds to a single request.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
