    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAvailability is the percentage of time a system is operational and accessible. Measured as uptime / (uptime + downtime). High availability (HA) is often 99.99% (four nines) or 99.999% (five nines). It is achieved through redundancy, failover, and monitoring.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain availability and how is it measured accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Availability is the percentage of time a system is operational and accessible.\n2. Measured as uptime / (uptime + downtime).\n3. High availability (HA) is often 99.99% (four nines) or 99.999% (five nines).\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Availability is the percentage of time a system is operational and accessible.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Availability is the percentage of time a system is operational and accessible.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
