    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAvailability is about whether the system is up. Reliability is about whether the system performs correctly (no errors, data corruption). A system can be available but unreliable (returns wrong data).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between availability and reliability and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Availability is about whether the system is up.\n2. Reliability is about whether the system performs correctly (no errors, data corruption).\n3. A system can be available but unreliable (returns wrong data).\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Availability is about whether the system is up.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Availability is about whether the system is up.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
