    question: "What is the difference between vertical and horizontal partitioning?",
    slug: "vertical-vs-horizontal-partitioning",
    shortDescription: "Compare partitioning methods.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nVertical partitioning splits tables by columns (e.g., separate profile and payment data). Horizontal partitioning (sharding) splits by rows based on a key. Both aim to reduce dataset size per server.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between vertical and horizontal partitioning and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Vertical partitioning splits tables by columns (e.g., separate profile and payment data).\n2. Horizontal partitioning (sharding) splits by rows based on a key.\n3. Both aim to reduce dataset size per server.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Vertical partitioning splits tables by columns (e.g., separate profile and payment data).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Vertical partitioning splits tables by columns (e.g., separate profile and payment data).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
