    slug: "capacity-estimation",
    shortDescription: "Explain capacity planning.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nEstimate traffic (QPS), storage (data growth), bandwidth, and memory. Use metrics: daily active users, average request size, read/write ratio. Calculate required resources and plan for scaling.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you estimate system capacity, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Estimate traffic (QPS), storage (data growth), bandwidth, and memory.\n2. Use metrics: daily active users, average request size, read/write ratio.\n3. Calculate required resources and plan for scaling.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Estimate traffic (QPS), storage (data growth), bandwidth, and memory.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Estimate traffic (QPS), storage (data growth), bandwidth, and memory.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
