    slug: "stateless-vs-stateful",
    shortDescription: "Compare service types.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nStateless services do not store client session data; each request is independent. They are easier to scale horizontally. Stateful services store session data (e.g., in-memory) and require sticky sessions or distributed caches.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between stateless and stateful services and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Stateless services do not store client session data; each request is independent.\n2. They are easier to scale horizontally.\n3. Stateful services store session data (e.g., in-memory) and require sticky sessions or distributed caches.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Stateless services do not store client session data; each request is independent.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Stateless services do not store client session data; each request is independent.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
